## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to implement a pragmatic validation gate with dead-letter queue (DLQ) routing in KCNA, along with careful offset management to preserve per-message semantics in a multi-partition, multi-topic environment. It also tests understanding of canary test design for validating end-to-end behavior in production-like scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- Per-message schema validation in KCNA pipelines
- Dead-letter routing and sink isolation for failed records
- Offset management: commit-after-acknowledge pattern for reliability
- Canary test design with mixed valid and invalid records

## Code Example

```javascript
// KCNA validation gate
function validateSchema(msg) {
  const v = msg.value;
  if (!v || typeof v.userId !== 'string' || typeof v.eventType !== 'string' || typeof v.timestamp !== 'number') {
    return { valid: false, error: 'Schema validation failed' };
  }
  return { valid: true };
}

// Process each message
async function processMessage(msg, { producer, commitOffsets }) {
  const validation = validateSchema(msg);

  if (validation.valid) {
    await producer.send({ topic: 'events-validated', messages: [msg] });
    await commitOffsets([{ topic: 'events', partition: msg.partition, offset: msg.offset + 1 }]);
  } else {
    await producer.send({ topic: 'dead_letter', messages: [{ value: { original: msg.value, error: validation.error } }] });
    await commitOffsets([{ topic: 'events', partition: msg.partition, offset: msg.offset + 1 }]);
  }
}

// Canary test scenario
const testRecords = [
  { topic: 'events', partition: 0, value: { userId: 'u123', eventType: 'click', timestamp: 1700000000 } },
  { topic: 'events', partition: 0, value: { userId: 'u456', eventType: 'view' } },
  { topic: 'events', partition: 1, value: { userId: 'u789', eventType: 'purchase', timestamp: 1700000001 } },
];
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a scalable, privacy-preserving per-tenant DP aggregator in a streaming KCNA pipeline, including budget accounting, isolation, and test strategy for real-world fleet telemetry.

## Key Concepts
- Differential privacy in streaming; per-tenant budgeting; windowing; sensitivity; noise distribution; composition.
- Data model: tenant-id, vehicle readings, hourly windows, sums/counted values.
- System design: sandboxed operators, per-tenant isolation, watermarking, backpressure, fault tolerance.
- Testing: DP correctness, privacy accounting, performance under burst, end-to-end latency.

## Code Example
```javascript
function laplace(scale) {
  const u = Math.random() - 0.5;
  return -scale * Math.sign(u) * Math.log(1 - 2 * Math.abs(u));
}
function addDpNoise(sum, count, epsilon) {
  const noisySum = sum + laplace(1 / epsilon);
  const noisyCount = count + Math.max(1, Math.round(laplace(1 / epsilon)));
  return noisySum / Math.max(1, noisyCount);
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt epsilon per-tenant based on usage or SLAs? 
- How would you verify privacy loss in production with audit trails and budget exhaustion alerts?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical schema evolution handling in KCNA beginners: safe deserialization by versioning, backward compatibility, DLQ handling, and testability with canaries.

## Key Concepts
- Schema versioning
- In-memory deserializer registry
- Backward compatibility
- Dead-letter routing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal example
type Message = { payload: any };
type Deser = (payload: any)=>any;

const registry = new Map<number, Deser>();
registry.set(1, (p)=> ({id: p.id, t: p.time}));
registry.set(2, (p)=> ({id: p.id, ts: p.time, meta: p.meta}));

function deserialize(record: { payload: any }) {
  const v = Number(record.payload?.schema_version ?? 1);
  const deser = registry.get(v) ?? registry.get(1);
  if (!deser) throw new Error('No deserializer');
  return deser(record.payload);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to support field renames without changing consumer code?
- How would you test compatibility across v1 and v2 payloads in a canary run?

